The mechanism is above; this is the part you type. Both are short on purpose: the idea you can re-derive under pressure, the exact shape you cannot.

```ts caption="What you ship: the library call, plus the line everyone forgets"
const search = useMemo(() => debounce(runSearch, 300, { maxWait: 1000 }), [runSearch]);

useEffect(() => () => search.cancel(), [search]); // nothing fires after the route changes
```

- `debounce(fn, wait)` returns a function; the options go third: `{ leading, trailing, maxWait }`.
- `cancel()` and `flush()` hang off the returned function, and `cancel()` belongs in the cleanup — otherwise a pending call runs after unmount.
- Memoize the debounced function (or keep it in a ref): re-created every render, it is a new timer per render and no debouncing at all.

```tsx caption="What you write on the whiteboard: setTimeout in an effect, cleared on the next change"
function useDebounced(value: string, wait = 300) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), wait);
    return () => clearTimeout(id); // the previous timer dies before the next one starts
  }, [value, wait]);

  return debounced;
}
```

Three things the interviewer is watching: the timer id is captured **inside** the effect, the cleanup clears exactly that id, and `value` and `wait` are in the deps — the cleanup only runs because the effect re-runs. For a callback instead of a value the shape is the block above (`useMemo` + `cancel()`); pick one and say why.

Practise typing both without looking. In the round the failure is never the concept — it is where `clearTimeout` goes.

