Build a **lookup index** once, then scan the other collection and query it by key. Two nested loops compare every pair, costing O(n·m); a `Map` index uses the collection's keys and reduces the work to O(n + m) on average, with O(n) extra space. This is a join or enrichment, not deduplication: decide what happens when keys repeat, whether the first or last record wins, and what a missing match means. `Map` keys use SameValueZero and object keys use identity, so use a stable primitive such as an id when records came from separate responses.

```js caption="Write the index and enrichment by hand; explain O(n·m) versus O(n + m)"
function enrich(orders, users) {
  const userById = new Map(users.map((user) => [user.id, user]));
  return orders.map((order) => ({
    ...order,
    user: userById.get(order.userId) ?? null,
  }));
}

// Nested scan: O(orders.length * users.length)
// Map index + scan: O(orders.length + users.length) average
```

